<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The app theme, and the ONLY place that tells WebView which palette the page should paint.

     Why this file exists at all: the companion IS a WebView (the cockpit page is the UI), and
     WebView does not read the system's night setting; it reads the theme's android:isLightTheme.
     Per Android's "Darken web content in WebView": "WebView always sets prefers-color-scheme
     according to isLightTheme. If isLightTheme is true or not specified, then prefers-color-scheme
     is light; otherwise, it is dark."

     The app used to point straight at @android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar, whose
     isLightTheme is true, so the page was pinned to the light palette for good while the native
     toolbar (Ui.java, which reads Configuration.UI_MODE_NIGHT_MASK) followed the system. Measured
     on Android 16 with the system in night mode: matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)') came
     back false and the page's bg token was #f4f6f9 (light) under a dark toolbar.

     So: one theme per night qualifier, identical except for isLightTheme. Both parent the LIGHT
     platform theme on purpose, so the only difference is the flag and the app's own widget styling
     (and the window background) does not change behind Ui.java's back. Add no colors here.

     API note: android:isLightTheme landed in API 29 (minSdk here is 26). On older devices the
     attribute is simply unused; no values-v29 copy is needed. -->
<resources>
    <style name="Theme.Agentus" parent="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:isLightTheme">true</item>
    </style>
</resources>
